{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c511","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a0adcc-09d6-79d1-9b97-6e93479b8dc1"},"sequence":227,"recorded_at":1780531400000449,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"410e85d5-c650-4a55-ae65-44f1be1f0a3d","payload_type":"task.lifecycle.output","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"task_lifecycle","command_id":"410e85d5-c650-4a55-ae65-44f1be1f0a3d","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"410e85d5-c650-4a55-ae65-44f1be1f0a3d"},"task_stream":{"kind":"task","id":"01a0adcc-92f4-7c40-870a-6b937b13f74e"},"task_id":"01a0adcc-92f4-7c40-870a-6b937b13f74e","event":{"kind":"output","task_id":"01a0adcc-92f4-7c40-870a-6b937b13f74e","chunk":"Read text file `internal/specdoc/SPEC.v0.6.0.md`.\n(9942 line(s) above)\n9943|### 13.12 Failure and recovery matrix\n9944|\n9945|| Failure | Required behavior | Recovery |\n9946|| --- | --- | --- |\n9947|| SSH disconnect during record/chunk transfer | Keep verified staging and immutable committed objects; no partial materialization | Retry <code>ax sync --resume TRANSFER_ID</code> |\n9948|| Invalid digest/schema | Quarantine object, stop affected sync, identify peer/object without payload logging | Repair source or remove peer authorization; fetch a known-good object |\n9949|| Workspace divergence | No destination write | Diff, copy, worktree, or explicit managed-replica replacement |\n9950|| Provider quiesce cannot prove full idle | Graceful stop/takeover fails; owner unchanged | Wait/retry or explicit force action |\n9951|| Provider exits but store handles remain open | No post-stop/superseding checkpoint or takeover lease | Wait boundedly, diagnose, or force with unclean marker |\n9952|| Destination disk full | Retain existing destination; staging can remain partial | Free space and resume |\n9953|| Atomic rename blocked | Roll back or retain staging; never in-place overwrite | Close handles and retry |\n9954|| Plugin crash/invalid stdout | Record provider-host error; do not infer capability or success | Run doctor, repair/upgrade plugin, retry |\n9955|| Epoch-1 launch fails before first checkpoint | Never report stopped/resumable or invent a checkpoint | Reconcile and retry the same bootstrap identity, establish the first checkpoint, or prove closure and record non-resumable bootstrap abort |\n9956|| Task-board bridge missing/incompatible | Do not inspect private state; task-board portability disabled | Install compatible task-board contract |\n9957|| Task-board import/open/adopt fails before new lease | Old owner remains authoritative in graceful flow | Roll back dormant import and retry |\n9958|| Task-board adopt fails after new lease | Destination remains stopped owner | Retry adopt/resume or newer explicit transfer |\n9959|| Owner-resume response is lost | Preserve prepared journal and do not start a second process/manager | Reconcile materialization plus provider/bridge status, then finalize, roll back pre-activation, or explicitly stop post-activation |\n9960|| Antigravity backend cannot resolve UUID | No fake resume or blank replacement | Authenticate correct realm, use provider-native fork if possible, or report unsupported |\n9961|| Muse cron/background state cannot be fenced | Portable takeover/fork unavailable | Stop locally or wait for a supported adapter/version |\n9962|| Concurrent force takeovers | Deterministic lease winner; preserve losing history | Stop losing process and reconcile divergent branch |\n9963|| Old owner reconnects after force | Reject its lower/losing fencing token from authoritative sync/resume | Park/stop stale process; inspect divergent events |\n9964|| SQLite index corrupt | Do not sync database | Move corrupt index to local diagnostics and rebuild from immutable truth |\n9965|| Operator interrupt | Preserve transaction journal and report whether authority changed | Retry same operation ID; inspect status before another takeover |\n9966|\n9967|### 13.13 Crash/restart outcome gate\n9968|\n9969|Every implementation MUST inject a crash and a clean process restart at every\n9970|boundary in the registry below for each applicable direct and task-board path.\n9971|After restart, the recovery evaluator MUST classify the operation into exactly\n9972|one of these three normative outcomes:\n9973|\n9974|| Outcome | Exclusive normative meaning | Required evidence |\n9975|| --- | --- | --- |\n9976|| <code>safe_retry</code> | The same logical operation can continue or return its recorded result using every caller-stable operation/materialization/transaction/bridge ID and byte-identical immutable input. The winning lease and the exact persisted Provider Identity Record or task-board manager reference/Ax Binding are unchanged. A retry MUST reconcile an uncertain external effect before issuing it again and MUST NOT allocate another process, manager, native handle, lease epoch, staging authority, or transaction root. | The pre-crash durable receipt or journal phase, the post-restart status result for every possibly executed provider/bridge/external effect, the unchanged winning lease, the unchanged native identity/binding, and the single returned or advanced durable result. |\n9977|| <code>explicit_rollback</code> | Recovery executes an allowed abort, rollback, or proven closure before owner activation. It restores every affected predecessor or removes only inert fresh staging. The terminal rollback/abort result is durable and visible through the existing journal, event, CLI/status, and audit surfaces; recovery MUST NOT silently discard evidence or describe an implicit byte reversal as success. Ownership changes only through an already specified explicit lease transition and never rolls back merely because bytes were rolled back. | A terminal <code>rolled_back</code>, bootstrap-abort, handoff-abort, or equivalent existing durable result; predecessor/closure verification; unchanged or explicitly advanced winning lease as required by the flow; no live provider/manager effect; and operator-visible reason/remediation. |\n9978|| <code>recoverable_parked_state</code> | Recovery cannot yet prove safe replay or legal rollback, so it fails closed. The authoritative lease, exact operation IDs, checkpoint/native identity, last durable phase, and unresolved external-effect evidence remain recoverable; input and activation are blocked. The lifecycle projection MUST be <code>parked</code>, <code>failed</code>, or a stopped owner as already allowed by the flow, and status/doctor MUST expose the blocking reason and the same-operation retry, explicit stop/transfer, or reconciliation action. | Durable journal/event/lease/checkpoint facts sufficient to resume reconciliation, a status/doctor failure naming the ambiguous or blocked effect, proof that no losing or additional owner accepts input, and proof that no new native session or manager was allocated. |\n9979|\n9980|These outcomes are mutually exclusive. <code>safe_retry</code> requires an\n9981|unchanged exact identity and a replayable or already recorded effect, so it\n9982|cannot also have a terminal rollback/abort fact or an unresolved activation.\n9983|<code>explicit_rollback</code> requires a visible terminal reversal/closure and\n9984|no live activated effect, so it cannot be retry success or parked ambiguity.\n9985|<code>recoverable_parked_state</code> is selected only when neither of the first\n9986|two predicates is proven. They are collectively exhaustive: missing, stale,\n9987|contradictory, unreachable, or ambiguous evidence MUST select\n9988|<code>recoverable_parked_state</code>; an implementation MUST NOT invent a\n9989|fourth recovery outcome or report an unclassified successful restart.\n9990|\n9991|The gate is evaluated per logical session and per migration cohort. Every row\n9992|means both “after the named phase's durable write, before the next phase” and\n9993|“after the named external effect may have happened, before its result is\n9994|durable” when that effect exists. A row whose phase is skipped by a valid plan\n9995|is not applicable to that plan; every boundary between the remaining adjacent\n9996|phases still is. The conformance record MUST name the boundary ID, path,\n9997|operation IDs, pre/post durable facts, external effect and status probe,\n9998|winning lease before/after, native identity/binding before/after, selected\n9999|outcome, and the evidence satisfying that outcome.\n10000|\n10001|| Boundary IDs | Required injection boundary and applicable paths |\n10002|| --- | --- |\n10003|| <code>CR-LAUNCH-D-01..05</code> | Direct bootstrap: after session/lease/event persistence; terminal creation; provider process start; Provider Identity persistence; capture/object validation, each before the next Section 13.1 phase or first-checkpoint publication. |\n10004|| <code>CR-LAUNCH-TB-01..03</code> | Task-board bootstrap: after session/lease/event persistence; bridge launch may have returned a manager reference; bridge export may have returned a bundle/proof, each before the next Section 13.2 durable phase or first-checkpoint publication. |\n10005|| <code>CR-SYNC-01..07</code> | Sync: after inventory exchange; immutable union; missing-object selection; staged chunk progress; immutable-object commit; passive <code>materialize.prepare</code>/<code>commit</code>; dormant finalize, each before the next Section 13.3 phase or <code>sync.completed</code>. |\n10006|| <code>CR-MAT-01..08</code> | Every workspace/provider/task-board/composite materialization: before journal creation; after journal/prepare receipt; after transfer; after validation; after provider prepare or bridge import; after bridge open or host commit enters prepared; after owner activation may have occurred; after finalize/cleanup may have occurred but before its result/event is durable. This row applies independently to graceful takeover, force takeover, passive sync, owner resume, and fork. |\n10007|| <code>CR-GRACE-01..13</code> | Graceful takeover: after prepare; input block; quiescence proof; checkpoint capture; final sync; destination validation; source stop; closure-delta checkpoint; destination prepared materialization; destination lease persistence/union; direct resume or task-board activation; finalize; source park/resume-event publication, each before the next Section 13.6 phase. |\n10008|| <code>CR-FORCE-01..07</code> | Common force takeover: after refresh/pinned-cohort selection; divergence preservation; inert staging; final preflight; confirmation receipt; new lease persistence; winning-lease recomputation/events, each before the applicable direct/task-board activation path. |\n10009|| <code>CR-FORCE-D-01..05</code> | Direct force activation: after prepare; prepared commit/provider materialization; native discovery; plugin resume/process start; finalize may have occurred, each before the next Section 13.7 durable result/event. |\n10010|| <code>CR-FORCE-TB-01..04</code> | Task-board force activation: after prepare/journal creation; import/open/commit prepared; adopt may have occurred; resume/finalize may have occurred, each before the next Section 13.7 durable result/event. |\n10011|| <code>CR-FORK-01..08</code> | Fork: after ID allocation; topology/manifest projection persistence; new Session Record/lease persistence; provider fork plan or bundle selection; prepared materialization; direct identity/activation; task-board adopt/activation; finalize before exactly-once fork/resume events, each before the next Section 13.8 phase. |\n10012|| <code>CR-STOP-01..05</code> | Stop: after input block/quiesce; checkpoint/export capture; process/manager stop may have occurred; closure verification; stopped event/result persistence, each before the next Section 13.9 phase. Bootstrap abort uses the corresponding <code>CR-LAUNCH-*</code> boundary and the same outcome gate. |\n10013|| <code>CR-RESUME-01..06</code> | Owner resume: after owner/checkpoint/profile validation; prepare; prepared commit; exact native discovery or dormant manager reconciliation; provider/bridge activation may have occurred; finalize may have occurred before exactly-once <code>session.resumed</code>, each before the next Section 13.10 phase. |\n10014|| <code>CR-RESTORE-01..07</code> | Reboot restore: after index validation; prior-live-session enumeration; lease refresh; stale-owner marking; checkpoint/profile validation; wrapper recreation; auto-resume may have occurred before durable status/event reconciliation, each before the next Section 13.11 phase. |\n10015|\n10016|For every boundary, the gate MUST reject the run if two hosts or two native\n10017|processes/managers can both be treated as live or authoritative for the same\n10018|logical session. A losing or unfenced external continuation is not\n10019|<code>safe_retry</code>, even if the deterministic lease winner is known; it\n10020|MUST be fenced/stopped with visible divergent-history evidence or remain\n10021|<code>recoverable_parked_state</code>. The gate also MUST reject any recovery\n10022|that invokes a new-session launch, allocates a fresh native handle or manager\n10023|reference, relabels blank state, or resumes a different provider/account realm\n10024|in place of the exact persisted Provider Identity Record or task-board Ax\n10025|Binding. Such substitution is never a successful retry, rollback, or parked\n10026|recovery.\n10027|\n10028|Clone fault injection additionally uses <code>CR-CLONE-01..16</code> for,\n10029|in order: resolve; probe; inspect; snapshot; capture plan; raw capture;\n10030|normalize; canonical validation; projection/checkpoint plan; policy gate;\n10031|prepare; staged read-back; source/collision recheck; publish/live read-back;\n10032|finalize/lineage; and resume-plan/optional open. Each phase is injected after\n10033|its durable write and after any external effect but before its result is\n10034|durable. Finalization injection further covers Provider commit, durable commit\n10035|facts, fixed checkpoint inputs, target Checkpoint,\n10036|<code>checkpoint.created</code>, <code>clone.committed</code>, Lineage Receipt,\n10037|and G4. Every prefix selects exactly one Section 13.13 outcome and preserves\n10038|one operation, bundle, materialization, transaction, target Session, native\n10039|identity, lease, and Checkpoint. Rollback is forbidden after Provider commit.\n10040|\n10041|### 13.14 Cross-environment clone\n10042|\n10043|A clone is a derivation, never a move or fork alias. It MUST leave source\n10044|bytes, Session Record, provider ID, lease, workspace authority, task-board\n10045|binding, and native identity unchanged. It creates one new direct Session\n10046|Record 2.0.0, target workspace identity, native identity, and epoch-1 lease.\n10047|The target accepts no input until transaction, target Checkpoint, lineage, and\n10048|optional ordinary resume validation succeed.\n10049|\n10050|#### 13.14.1 Capture and canonical contracts\n10051|\n10052|Clone Raw Object Manifest 1.0.0 is closed and contains its\n10053|schema/version/omission-rule ID, operation ID, source Environment Tuple, and\n10054|sorted unique entries. Each entry has sanitized native\n10055|key, capture class, byte count, blob ID, Blob Descriptor ID, and extensions.\n10056|It contains no credential, runtime, lock, target, AX lease, or fabricated AX\n10057|Session/Provider identity.\n10058|\n10059|Clone Capture Manifest 1.0.0 contains exactly its schema/version/ID,\n10060|operation/bundle, source basis, source Environment Tuple, sanitized Native\n10061|Identity, capture-plan digest, Capture Boundary, Raw Object Manifest ID,\n10062|sorted Capture Items, exact excluded classes, core-derived\n(more lines below)","final_result":true}}}
{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c513","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a0adcc-09d6-79d1-9b97-6e93479b8dc1"},"sequence":228,"recorded_at":1780531400000451,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"410e85d5-c650-4a55-ae65-44f1be1f0a3d","payload_type":"task.lifecycle.completed","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"task_lifecycle","command_id":"410e85d5-c650-4a55-ae65-44f1be1f0a3d","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"410e85d5-c650-4a55-ae65-44f1be1f0a3d"},"task_stream":{"kind":"task","id":"01a0adcc-92f4-7c40-870a-6b937b13f74e"},"task_id":"01a0adcc-92f4-7c40-870a-6b937b13f74e","event":{"kind":"completed","task_id":"01a0adcc-92f4-7c40-870a-6b937b13f74e"}}}
{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c515","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a0adcc-09d6-79d1-9b97-6e93479b8dc1"},"sequence":229,"recorded_at":1780531400000453,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"410e85d5-c650-4a55-ae65-44f1be1f0a3d","payload_type":"tool.result","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"tool_result","command_id":"410e85d5-c650-4a55-ae65-44f1be1f0a3d","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"410e85d5-c650-4a55-ae65-44f1be1f0a3d"},"call_id":"call_01a0adcc8da370c78f22bc76aa230942","text":"Read text file `internal/specdoc/SPEC.v0.6.0.md`.\n(6016 line(s) above)\n6017|| <code>workspace_group_id</code> | UUIDv7 | Exact materialized group |\n6018|| <code>primary_session_id</code> | UUIDv7 | Session/checkpoint that drove this materialization |\n6019|| <code>source_checkpoint_id</code> | digest | Validated source checkpoint |\n6020|| <code>workspace_group_record_id</code> | digest | Unique Section 5.6 topology record |\n6021|| <code>workspace_manifest_id</code> | digest | Exact installed workspace-group root manifest |\n6022|| <code>plan_id</code> | digest | Executed Materialization Plan |\n6023|| <code>materialization_id</code> | UUIDv7 | Journal/RPC transaction that installed it |\n6024|| <code>destination</code> | Replica Destination | Closed shape below |\n6025|| <code>predecessor_marker_id</code> | digest or null | Prior current marker; null only for a newly managed path |\n6026|| <code>committed_at</code> | timestamp | Diagnostic commit time |\n6027|| <code>extensions</code> | object | Reverse-DNS machine-local extensions only |\n6028|\n6029|Replica Destination contains exactly\n6030|<code>logical_root:string[1..64]</code>,\n6031|<code>workspace_relative_path:path</code>, and\n6032|<code>resolved_path_fingerprint:digest</code>. The first two resolve through\n6033|the current host configuration. The fingerprint is SHA-256 of the UTF-8 bytes\n6034|<code>urn:ax:replica-path:1 NUL platform NUL canonical-absolute-path</code>,\n6035|where NUL is one zero byte and Windows path case/volume normalization follows\n6036|Section 3.2. It detects remapped configuration; it is routing evidence, not a\n6037|logical workspace identity.\n6038|\n6039|Normative marker fixture:\n6040|\n6041|~~~json\n6042|{\n6043|  \"schema\": \"urn:ax:schema:materialization-journal\",\n6044|  \"schema_version\": \"2.0.0\",\n6045|  \"document_kind\": \"managed_replica_marker\",\n6046|  \"marker_id\": \"sha256:385c71c7a29a43615c9d35ffb7c93ae20cd9419bbca461627048de575cade94c\",\n6047|  \"managed_replica_id\": \"0198f4c8-8e50-7f66-8f70-2234567890ab\",\n6048|  \"host_id\": \"0198f4c8-7d40-7e55-8e6f-1234567890ab\",\n6049|  \"platform\": \"linux\",\n6050|  \"workspace_group_id\": \"0198f4c8-5b20-7c33-8c4d-1234567890ab\",\n6051|  \"primary_session_id\": \"0198f4c8-3e70-7a11-8a2b-1234567890ab\",\n6052|  \"source_checkpoint_id\": \"sha256:e051996f51f13ace4f5cdebe1e30fd26fd5fe104cfd6e6a7f9f1206ba3819656\",\n6053|  \"workspace_group_record_id\": \"sha256:3b366ca989681c63323c5de6db28198796aa913947ad3cd9456fc6dcee62b743\",\n6054|  \"workspace_manifest_id\": \"sha256:a98ca90522b4de30e4aaaf9bf50529d09e15a817ffa67f94552fb313d1a1ad2e\",\n6055|  \"plan_id\": \"sha256:64644a5ad573d36c0c13f44f56ef25ab93cff33001ff2a3371b082603910f2dd\",\n6056|  \"materialization_id\": \"0198f4c8-c290-73aa-9374-1234567890ab\",\n6057|  \"destination\": {\n6058|    \"logical_root\": \"relux\",\n6059|    \"workspace_relative_path\": \"payments\",\n6060|    \"resolved_path_fingerprint\": \"sha256:244d99a3e794e9ea89b4e30429adfd6bc142c069cf0635576e010031f5b85ded\"\n6061|  },\n6062|  \"predecessor_marker_id\": null,\n6063|  \"committed_at\": \"2026-08-19T04:12:30.000Z\",\n6064|  \"extensions\": {}\n6065|}\n6066|~~~\n6067|\n6068|The marker is stored as owner-only JSON in both\n6069|<code>&lt;state&gt;/managed-replicas/&lt;managed-replica-id&gt;/markers/sha256/HH/REST.json</code>,\n6070|using <code>digest_path_v1(marker_id)</code> from Section 3.2,\n6071|and the atomically replaceable\n6072|<code>&lt;state&gt;/managed-replicas/&lt;managed-replica-id&gt;/current.json</code>.\n6073|The history file is create-new, fsynced, and never changed. The writer then\n6074|writes a same-directory temporary current file, fsyncs it, atomically renames\n6075|it, and fsyncs the parent where supported. The journal records the marker ID\n6076|only after the target bytes and history file are durable. SQLite indexes these\n6077|files and MUST be rebuildable by scanning them.\n6078|\n6079|A current marker is valid only when its self-ID, predecessor chain, host ID,\n6080|platform, workspace group and topology record, source checkpoint, plan,\n6081|materialization, configured logical-root resolution, and path fingerprint all\n6082|validate. A marker copied from another host or placed under another managed-\n6083|replica directory is never destination authority.\n6084|\n6085|For crash recovery, a <code>committing</code> journal with no current marker may\n6086|create the exact marker only after revalidating every plan authority,\n6087|predecessor, installed manifest byte, provider transaction, and task-board\n6088|state. Any mismatch rolls back when possible or fails integrity-closed; it\n6089|never writes a marker merely because the target path exists. A current marker\n6090|whose plan/checkpoint/materialization differs from the journal is\n6091|<code>MJ-CRASH-MARKER-MISMATCH</code>.\n6092|\n6093|Destination classification is deterministic:\n6094|\n6095|| Durable state | Classification/action |\n6096|| --- | --- |\n6097|| No marker; target absent | <code>absent</code> |\n6098|| No marker; target empty | <code>empty</code> |\n6099|| No marker; target nonempty | <code>unmanaged_nonempty</code> |\n6100|| Valid current marker resolves to this exact path and a fresh workspace capture equals <code>workspace_manifest_id</code> | <code>managed_unchanged</code> |\n6101|| Valid current marker resolves here but fresh content differs | <code>managed_divergent</code>; preserve both states |\n6102|| Marker digest, path fingerprint, predecessor chain, or referenced object is invalid | <code>integrity_failure</code>; report managed divergence diagnostically and mutate nothing |\n6103|\n6104|<code>MARKER-ABSENT</code>, <code>MARKER-MATCH</code>,\n6105|<code>MARKER-CONTENT-MISMATCH</code>, <code>MARKER-PATH-MISMATCH</code>, and\n6106|<code>MARKER-CRASH-RECONSTRUCT</code> are the five rows above plus the\n6107|crash-reconstruction rule and are normative fixtures. A reader that does not\n6108|support this major version MUST treat the path as managed-but-incompatible and\n6109|fail closed; migration follows Section 17 and MUST write a new marker linked\n6110|through <code>predecessor_marker_id</code>, never edit an old marker.\n6111|\n6112|### 10.7 Tombstone\n6113|\n6114|Tombstone schema <code>urn:ax:schema:tombstone</code> version\n6115|<code>1.0.0</code> is immutable. Its common closed shape is:\n6116|\n6117|| Field | Type | Constraint |\n6118|| --- | --- | --- |\n6119|| <code>schema</code> | string | Exact Tombstone schema identifier |\n6120|| <code>schema_version</code> | semver | Exact <code>1.0.0</code> |\n6121|| <code>tombstone_id</code> | digest | Canonical object digest |\n6122|| <code>scope</code> | enum | <code>session</code>, <code>workspace_entry</code>, <code>provider_snapshot</code>, or <code>managed_replica</code> |\n6123|| <code>subject_id</code> | UUIDv7 | Scope subject defined below |\n6124|| <code>authorizing_session_id</code> | UUIDv7 | Session whose owner authorizes the action |\n6125|| <code>basis_event_id</code> | digest | Immediately preceding authoritative event for the authorizing lease |\n6126|| <code>lease_epoch</code> | uint53 | Authorizing lease epoch |\n6127|| <code>lease_id</code> | UUIDv4 | Authorizing fencing token |\n6128|| <code>target</code> | tagged object | Exact scope-specific shape below |\n6129|| <code>created_by_host_id</code> | UUIDv7 | MUST equal the authorizing lease holder |\n6130|| <code>created_at</code> | timestamp | Diagnostic only |\n6131|| <code>extensions</code> | object | Reverse-DNS extension keys only |\n6132|\n6133|The <code>target</code> object is selected by <code>scope</code> and begins with\n6134|an equal <code>kind</code>. It contains exactly:\n6135|\n6136|| Scope | <code>subject_id</code> | Exact target members after <code>kind</code> |\n6137|| --- | --- | --- |\n6138|| <code>session</code> | Session ID | <code>session_id:UUIDv7</code>, <code>session_record_id:digest</code>, <code>predecessor_checkpoint_id:digest</code> |\n6139|| <code>workspace_entry</code> | Workspace Group ID | <code>workspace_group_id:UUIDv7</code>, <code>workspace_id:UUIDv7</code>, <code>predecessor_manifest_id:digest</code>, <code>relative_path:path</code>, <code>entry_type:directory&#124;file&#124;symlink&#124;hardlink</code>, <code>predecessor_entry_digest:digest</code> |\n6140|| <code>provider_snapshot</code> | Session ID | <code>session_id:UUIDv7</code>, <code>provider_identity_record_id:digest</code>, <code>predecessor_manifest_id:digest</code> |\n6141|| <code>managed_replica</code> | Workspace Group ID | <code>workspace_group_id:UUIDv7</code>, <code>target_host_id:UUIDv7</code>, <code>managed_replica_id:UUIDv7</code>, <code>logical_root:string[1..64]</code>, <code>destination_relative_path:path</code>, <code>predecessor_marker_id:digest</code>, <code>predecessor_checkpoint_id:digest</code> |\n6142|\n6143|Positive embedded target fixtures for the three variants not used by the full\n6144|example are:\n6145|\n6146|~~~json\n(more lines below)","correlation_facts":{"tool_name":"read_file","outcome":"success"}}}
{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c517","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a0adcc-09d6-79d1-9b97-6e93479b8dc1"},"sequence":230,"recorded_at":1780531400000455,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"410e85d5-c650-4a55-ae65-44f1be1f0a3d","payload_type":"tool.result","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"tool_result","command_id":"410e85d5-c650-4a55-ae65-44f1be1f0a3d","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"410e85d5-c650-4a55-ae65-44f1be1f0a3d"},"call_id":"call_01a0adcc8faf7613bb676990438c7a8a","text":"Read text file `internal/specdoc/SPEC.v0.6.0.md`.\n(9942 line(s) above)\n9943|### 13.12 Failure and recovery matrix\n9944|\n9945|| Failure | Required behavior | Recovery |\n9946|| --- | --- | --- |\n9947|| SSH disconnect during record/chunk transfer | Keep verified staging and immutable committed objects; no partial materialization | Retry <code>ax sync --resume TRANSFER_ID</code> |\n9948|| Invalid digest/schema | Quarantine object, stop affected sync, identify peer/object without payload logging | Repair source or remove peer authorization; fetch a known-good object |\n9949|| Workspace divergence | No destination write | Diff, copy, worktree, or explicit managed-replica replacement |\n9950|| Provider quiesce cannot prove full idle | Graceful stop/takeover fails; owner unchanged | Wait/retry or explicit force action |\n9951|| Provider exits but store handles remain open | No post-stop/superseding checkpoint or takeover lease | Wait boundedly, diagnose, or force with unclean marker |\n9952|| Destination disk full | Retain existing destination; staging can remain partial | Free space and resume |\n9953|| Atomic rename blocked | Roll back or retain staging; never in-place overwrite | Close handles and retry |\n9954|| Plugin crash/invalid stdout | Record provider-host error; do not infer capability or success | Run doctor, repair/upgrade plugin, retry |\n9955|| Epoch-1 launch fails before first checkpoint | Never report stopped/resumable or invent a checkpoint | Reconcile and retry the same bootstrap identity, establish the first checkpoint, or prove closure and record non-resumable bootstrap abort |\n9956|| Task-board bridge missing/incompatible | Do not inspect private state; task-board portability disabled | Install compatible task-board contract |\n9957|| Task-board import/open/adopt fails before new lease | Old owner remains authoritative in graceful flow | Roll back dormant import and retry |\n9958|| Task-board adopt fails after new lease | Destination remains stopped owner | Retry adopt/resume or newer explicit transfer |\n9959|| Owner-resume response is lost | Preserve prepared journal and do not start a second process/manager | Reconcile materialization plus provider/bridge status, then finalize, roll back pre-activation, or explicitly stop post-activation |\n9960|| Antigravity backend cannot resolve UUID | No fake resume or blank replacement | Authenticate correct realm, use provider-native fork if possible, or report unsupported |\n9961|| Muse cron/background state cannot be fenced | Portable takeover/fork unavailable | Stop locally or wait for a supported adapter/version |\n9962|| Concurrent force takeovers | Deterministic lease winner; preserve losing history | Stop losing process and reconcile divergent branch |\n9963|| Old owner reconnects after force | Reject its lower/losing fencing token from authoritative sync/resume | Park/stop stale process; inspect divergent events |\n9964|| SQLite index corrupt | Do not sync database | Move corrupt index to local diagnostics and rebuild from immutable truth |\n9965|| Operator interrupt | Preserve transaction journal and report whether authority changed | Retry same operation ID; inspect status before another takeover |\n9966|\n9967|### 13.13 Crash/restart outcome gate\n9968|\n9969|Every implementation MUST inject a crash and a clean process restart at every\n9970|boundary in the registry below for each applicable direct and task-board path.\n9971|After restart, the recovery evaluator MUST classify the operation into exactly\n9972|one of these three normative outcomes:\n9973|\n9974|| Outcome | Exclusive normative meaning | Required evidence |\n9975|| --- | --- | --- |\n9976|| <code>safe_retry</code> | The same logical operation can continue or return its recorded result using every caller-stable operation/materialization/transaction/bridge ID and byte-identical immutable input. The winning lease and the exact persisted Provider Identity Record or task-board manager reference/Ax Binding are unchanged. A retry MUST reconcile an uncertain external effect before issuing it again and MUST NOT allocate another process, manager, native handle, lease epoch, staging authority, or transaction root. | The pre-crash durable receipt or journal phase, the post-restart status result for every possibly executed provider/bridge/external effect, the unchanged winning lease, the unchanged native identity/binding, and the single returned or advanced durable result. |\n9977|| <code>explicit_rollback</code> | Recovery executes an allowed abort, rollback, or proven closure before owner activation. It restores every affected predecessor or removes only inert fresh staging. The terminal rollback/abort result is durable and visible through the existing journal, event, CLI/status, and audit surfaces; recovery MUST NOT silently discard evidence or describe an implicit byte reversal as success. Ownership changes only through an already specified explicit lease transition and never rolls back merely because bytes were rolled back. | A terminal <code>rolled_back</code>, bootstrap-abort, handoff-abort, or equivalent existing durable result; predecessor/closure verification; unchanged or explicitly advanced winning lease as required by the flow; no live provider/manager effect; and operator-visible reason/remediation. |\n9978|| <code>recoverable_parked_state</code> | Recovery cannot yet prove safe replay or legal rollback, so it fails closed. The authoritative lease, exact operation IDs, checkpoint/native identity, last durable phase, and unresolved external-effect evidence remain recoverable; input and activation are blocked. The lifecycle projection MUST be <code>parked</code>, <code>failed</code>, or a stopped owner as already allowed by the flow, and status/doctor MUST expose the blocking reason and the same-operation retry, explicit stop/transfer, or reconciliation action. | Durable journal/event/lease/checkpoint facts sufficient to resume reconciliation, a status/doctor failure naming the ambiguous or blocked effect, proof that no losing or additional owner accepts input, and proof that no new native session or manager was allocated. |\n9979|\n9980|These outcomes are mutually exclusive. <code>safe_retry</code> requires an\n9981|unchanged exact identity and a replayable or already recorded effect, so it\n9982|cannot also have a terminal rollback/abort fact or an unresolved activation.\n9983|<code>explicit_rollback</code> requires a visible terminal reversal/closure and\n9984|no live activated effect, so it cannot be retry success or parked ambiguity.\n9985|<code>recoverable_parked_state</code> is selected only when neither of the first\n9986|two predicates is proven. They are collectively exhaustive: missing, stale,\n9987|contradictory, unreachable, or ambiguous evidence MUST select\n9988|<code>recoverable_parked_state</code>; an implementation MUST NOT invent a\n9989|fourth recovery outcome or report an unclassified successful restart.\n9990|\n9991|The gate is evaluated per logical session and per migration cohort. Every row\n9992|means both “after the named phase's durable write, before the next phase” and\n9993|“after the named external effect may have happened, before its result is\n9994|durable” when that effect exists. A row whose phase is skipped by a valid plan\n9995|is not applicable to that plan; every boundary between the remaining adjacent\n9996|phases still is. The conformance record MUST name the boundary ID, path,\n9997|operation IDs, pre/post durable facts, external effect and status probe,\n9998|winning lease before/after, native identity/binding before/after, selected\n9999|outcome, and the evidence satisfying that outcome.\n10000|\n10001|| Boundary IDs | Required injection boundary and applicable paths |\n10002|| --- | --- |\n10003|| <code>CR-LAUNCH-D-01..05</code> | Direct bootstrap: after session/lease/event persistence; terminal creation; provider process start; Provider Identity persistence; capture/object validation, each before the next Section 13.1 phase or first-checkpoint publication. |\n10004|| <code>CR-LAUNCH-TB-01..03</code> | Task-board bootstrap: after session/lease/event persistence; bridge launch may have returned a manager reference; bridge export may have returned a bundle/proof, each before the next Section 13.2 durable phase or first-checkpoint publication. |\n10005|| <code>CR-SYNC-01..07</code> | Sync: after inventory exchange; immutable union; missing-object selection; staged chunk progress; immutable-object commit; passive <code>materialize.prepare</code>/<code>commit</code>; dormant finalize, each before the next Section 13.3 phase or <code>sync.completed</code>. |\n10006|| <code>CR-MAT-01..08</code> | Every workspace/provider/task-board/composite materialization: before journal creation; after journal/prepare receipt; after transfer; after validation; after provider prepare or bridge import; after bridge open or host commit enters prepared; after owner activation may have occurred; after finalize/cleanup may have occurred but before its result/event is durable. This row applies independently to graceful takeover, force takeover, passive sync, owner resume, and fork. |\n10007|| <code>CR-GRACE-01..13</code> | Graceful takeover: after prepare; input block; quiescence proof; checkpoint capture; final sync; destination validation; source stop; closure-delta checkpoint; destination prepared materialization; destination lease persistence/union; direct resume or task-board activation; finalize; source park/resume-event publication, each before the next Section 13.6 phase. |\n10008|| <code>CR-FORCE-01..07</code> | Common force takeover: after refresh/pinned-cohort selection; divergence preservation; inert staging; final preflight; confirmation receipt; new lease persistence; winning-lease recomputation/events, each before the applicable direct/task-board activation path. |\n10009|| <code>CR-FORCE-D-01..05</code> | Direct force activation: after prepare; prepared commit/provider materialization; native discovery; plugin resume/process start; finalize may have occurred, each before the next Section 13.7 durable result/event. |\n10010|| <code>CR-FORCE-TB-01..04</code> | Task-board force activation: after prepare/journal creation; import/open/commit prepared; adopt may have occurred; resume/finalize may have occurred, each before the next Section 13.7 durable result/event. |\n10011|| <code>CR-FORK-01..08</code> | Fork: after ID allocation; topology/manifest projection persistence; new Session Record/lease persistence; provider fork plan or bundle selection; prepared materialization; direct identity/activation; task-board adopt/activation; finalize before exactly-once fork/resume events, each before the next Section 13.8 phase. |\n10012|| <code>CR-STOP-01..05</code> | Stop: after input block/quiesce; checkpoint/export capture; process/manager stop may have occurred; closure verification; stopped event/result persistence, each before the next Section 13.9 phase. Bootstrap abort uses the corresponding <code>CR-LAUNCH-*</code> boundary and the same outcome gate. |\n10013|| <code>CR-RESUME-01..06</code> | Owner resume: after owner/checkpoint/profile validation; prepare; prepared commit; exact native discovery or dormant manager reconciliation; provider/bridge activation may have occurred; finalize may have occurred before exactly-once <code>session.resumed</code>, each before the next Section 13.10 phase. |\n10014|| <code>CR-RESTORE-01..07</code> | Reboot restore: after index validation; prior-live-session enumeration; lease refresh; stale-owner marking; checkpoint/profile validation; wrapper recreation; auto-resume may have occurred before durable status/event reconciliation, each before the next Section 13.11 phase. |\n10015|\n10016|For every boundary, the gate MUST reject the run if two hosts or two native\n10017|processes/managers can both be treated as live or authoritative for the same\n10018|logical session. A losing or unfenced external continuation is not\n10019|<code>safe_retry</code>, even if the deterministic lease winner is known; it\n10020|MUST be fenced/stopped with visible divergent-history evidence or remain\n10021|<code>recoverable_parked_state</code>. The gate also MUST reject any recovery\n10022|that invokes a new-session launch, allocates a fresh native handle or manager\n10023|reference, relabels blank state, or resumes a different provider/account realm\n10024|in place of the exact persisted Provider Identity Record or task-board Ax\n10025|Binding. Such substitution is never a successful retry, rollback, or parked\n10026|recovery.\n10027|\n10028|Clone fault injection additionally uses <code>CR-CLONE-01..16</code> for,\n10029|in order: resolve; probe; inspect; snapshot; capture plan; raw capture;\n10030|normalize; canonical validation; projection/checkpoint plan; policy gate;\n10031|prepare; staged read-back; source/collision recheck; publish/live read-back;\n10032|finalize/lineage; and resume-plan/optional open. Each phase is injected after\n10033|its durable write and after any external effect but before its result is\n10034|durable. Finalization injection further covers Provider commit, durable commit\n10035|facts, fixed checkpoint inputs, target Checkpoint,\n10036|<code>checkpoint.created</code>, <code>clone.committed</code>, Lineage Receipt,\n10037|and G4. Every prefix selects exactly one Section 13.13 outcome and preserves\n10038|one operation, bundle, materialization, transaction, target Session, native\n10039|identity, lease, and Checkpoint. Rollback is forbidden after Provider commit.\n10040|\n10041|### 13.14 Cross-environment clone\n10042|\n10043|A clone is a derivation, never a move or fork alias. It MUST leave source\n10044|bytes, Session Record, provider ID, lease, workspace authority, task-board\n10045|binding, and native identity unchanged. It creates one new direct Session\n10046|Record 2.0.0, target workspace identity, native identity, and epoch-1 lease.\n10047|The target accepts no input until transaction, target Checkpoint, lineage, and\n10048|optional ordinary resume validation succeed.\n10049|\n10050|#### 13.14.1 Capture and canonical contracts\n10051|\n10052|Clone Raw Object Manifest 1.0.0 is closed and contains its\n10053|schema/version/omission-rule ID, operation ID, source Environment Tuple, and\n10054|sorted unique entries. Each entry has sanitized native\n10055|key, capture class, byte count, blob ID, Blob Descriptor ID, and extensions.\n10056|It contains no credential, runtime, lock, target, AX lease, or fabricated AX\n10057|Session/Provider identity.\n10058|\n10059|Clone Capture Manifest 1.0.0 contains exactly its schema/version/ID,\n10060|operation/bundle, source basis, source Environment Tuple, sanitized Native\n10061|Identity, capture-plan digest, Capture Boundary, Raw Object Manifest ID,\n10062|sorted Capture Items, exact excluded classes, core-derived\n(more lines below)","correlation_facts":{"tool_name":"read_file","outcome":"success"}}}
