{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000cdab","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a0adcc-09d6-79d1-9b97-6e93479b8dc1"},"sequence":1328,"recorded_at":1780531400002651,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"410e85d5-c650-4a55-ae65-44f1be1f0a3d","payload_type":"tool.result","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"tool_result","command_id":"410e85d5-c650-4a55-ae65-44f1be1f0a3d","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"410e85d5-c650-4a55-ae65-44f1be1f0a3d"},"call_id":"call_01a0adcfe3837246a1a1024e5e54e1f5","text":"Read text file `internal/specdoc/SPEC.v0.6.0.md`.\n(223 line(s) above)\n224|### 1.6 Common data rules\n225|\n226|Every complete normative schema object MUST contain <code>schema</code> and\n227|<code>schema_version</code>. Protocol envelopes instead contain\n228|<code>protocol</code> and <code>protocol_version</code> and MAY carry a\n229|schema-versioned body. A code block explicitly labeled as an embedded object or\n230|success body is a fragment of its named containing contract and therefore does\n231|not repeat the containing schema/protocol members.\n232|\n233|The common logical data model has these rules:\n234|\n235|- map keys MUST be UTF-8 strings and MUST be unique;\n236|- text MUST be valid UTF-8 and SHOULD be NFC-normalized;\n237|- floating-point numbers, NaN, Infinity, non-string map keys, and duplicate\n238|  keys are forbidden;\n239|- JSON integers MUST be mathematically integral and lie in the interoperable\n240|  safe-integer interval <code>[-9007199254740991, 9007199254740991]</code>;\n241|- the type name <code>uint53</code> means a JSON integer in\n242|  <code>[0, 9007199254740991]</code>; the current contract set does not use JSON numbers\n243|  for a wider unsigned domain;\n244|- a field explicitly typed <code>decimal_uint64</code> is instead a JSON string\n245|  matching <code>0|[1-9][0-9]{0,19}</code> whose numeric value is at most\n246|  <code>18446744073709551615</code>; leading plus signs and leading zeroes are\n247|  forbidden;\n248|- bytes MUST be represented as a content-addressed blob or unpadded base64url;\n249|- timestamps MUST be real UTC RFC 3339 calendar instants with at least\n250|  millisecond precision; a string that matches the lexical grammar but names an\n251|  impossible date is invalid;\n252|- durations MUST be integer milliseconds;\n253|- enumerations are case-sensitive lower snake case;\n254|- identifiers created by <code>ax</code> MUST be canonical lowercase UUIDv7\n255|  strings unless a field explicitly defines a digest identifier; fields typed\n256|  UUIDv4 MUST be canonical lowercase RFC 4122 variant UUIDv4 strings;\n257|- SHA-256 digest identifiers MUST use <code>sha256:</code> followed by exactly\n258|  64 lowercase hexadecimal characters; and\n259|- platform-neutral relative paths MUST use forward slashes, MUST NOT begin with\n260|  a slash or drive prefix, MUST NOT contain an empty segment, <code>.</code>,\n261|  <code>..</code>, a NUL byte, or an encoded path separator.\n262|\n263|<code>base64url</code> means the canonical RFC 4648 URL-safe alphabet\n264|<code>[A-Za-z0-9_-]</code> with no padding and no whitespace; decoding then\n265|re-encoding MUST reproduce the string byte-for-byte. The compact type\n266|<code>base64url-256+</code> uses that encoding for 32–512 bytes and is reserved\n267|for random machine-local control/rollback tokens. A protocol field carrying\n268|payload bytes uses its separately declared decoded-size limit instead.\n269|\n270|<code>absolute-path</code> means a 1–32,767 character, NUL-free path that is\n271|absolute and lexically normalized for the platform named by its containing\n272|request: POSIX-rooted on macOS/Linux/WSL2, or drive-qualified/UNC on native\n273|Windows. It contains no <code>.</code> or <code>..</code> segment. Receivers\n274|still resolve symlinks/reparse points beneath an allowed root before mutation.\n275|An absolute path is machine-local routing data and never a logical identity or\n276|replicated source-path key.\n277|\n278|Immutable objects MAY be encoded as UTF-8 JSON or CBOR using the core\n279|deterministic encoding requirements of RFC 8949 Section 4.2.1. Their\n280|identity MUST be the SHA-256 digest of the RFC 8785 JSON Canonicalization\n281|Scheme (JCS) form of the logical object with its schema-defined self-identity\n282|field omitted. Those\n283|fields are <code>record_id</code>, <code>event_id</code>,\n284|<code>checkpoint_id</code>, <code>descriptor_id</code>,\n285|<code>manifest_id</code>, <code>plan_id</code>, <code>tombstone_id</code>, or\n286|<code>ack_id</code>, <code>bundle_id</code>, or <code>marker_id</code>, as\n287|applicable, plus <code>observation_id</code>, <code>batch_id</code>,\n288|<code>lineage_link_id</code>, <code>annotation_id</code>,\n289|<code>profile_id</code>, <code>job_request_id</code>,\n290|<code>job_receipt_id</code>, and <code>directory_receipt_id</code> for the\n291|directory contracts. Each schema names exactly one self field; the namespaces\n292|are total and disjoint. Section 10.3 separately defines a\n293|<code>chunk_id</code> as the digest of raw chunk bytes; mutable journals and\n294|observation streams are not identity-addressed objects. A CBOR decoder MUST\n295|reject values outside the common logical data model and MUST produce the same\n296|canonical JSON digest. This rule prevents JSON and CBOR encodings of one object\n297|from creating two identities.\n298|\n(more lines below)","correlation_facts":{"tool_name":"read_file","outcome":"success"}}}
